@* Introduction.

\ID{$Id$}

Random number routines for degas.  We attempt to satisfy four objectives
with these routines: (1) they should produce random numbers uniformly
distributed in $(0,1)$; (2) they should be fast; (3) they should be
portable; (4) it should be easy to run many independent sequences of random
numbers.

The routines are based on the subtractive method of described in by Knuth
\cite{knuth2}, pp.~171--172, based on a generator by G. J. Mitchell and D.
P. Moore.  This method is used by Knuth in the Metafont program
\cite{knuth86}.  It is also supplied as part of the random number
generators provided with BSD Unix \cite{random3}.  The method provides
random numbers at the cost of a one or two additions (or subtractions).
These additions do not cause bits to be lost and so the same results can be
obtained on different computers.  By calculating the random numbers in
batches and storing the results in an array, these additions vectorize.
Accessing a random number then involves checking whether the array of
random numbers needs filling, returning the next element of the array, and
incrementing an index into the array.  A separate routine for accessing the
random number array which avoids the check is provided.  This allows do
loops involving random numbers to vectorize.

The period of this random number generator is at least $2^k-1 \approx
10^{19}$ (for $k=63$).  (This is the period of the least significant bit of
the sequence.  The actual period is many times larger.)  Furthermore each
seed will (probably) give an entirely different sequence.  (Contrast this
with the multiplicative method, where specifying the seed merely gives the
starting point on a single sequence of numbers.)

The random numbers routines are initialized with a 47-bit seed.  A separate
linear congruential random generator is provided to generate these seeds.
This is {\it not} optimized for speed.  However, it lets the user
conveniently initialize many independent random number sequences.

This has been replaced by a initialization using the high 47 bits of a
70-bit linear congruential random number generator.

@m FILE 'random.web'

@I macros.hweb

@i random.hweb

@* External interface to random.

The state of the random number generator is stored in |rn_args(tag)|,
where |tag| is an arbitrary tag.  This should not be altered by any
external routine.  |rn_decl(tag)| declares |rn_args(tag)| and, in
a top-level routine, allocates space for them.

The externally callable routines are:

|call random_init_old(seed,rn_args(tag))| which initializes the random number
using a two-element integer array |seed[0:1]|.  |seed| must satisfy
|0<=seed[0]<2^24| and |0<=seed[1]<2^23|.

|call random_seed_old(seed)| initializes a two-element integer array
|seed[0:1]| ``randomly'', based on the current time and the processor id.
The array |seed| is suitable for passing to |random_init_old|.

|call next_seed_old(n,seed,newseed)| advances the seed $n$ step forward.  This
allows independent threads of random numbers to be initialized.

|call random_init(seed,rn_args(tag))| which initializes the random
number using a four-element integer array |seed[0:3]|.  Each element of
|seed| must satisfy |0<=seed[i]<2^14|.

|call set_random_seed(seed)| initializes a four-element integer array
|seed[0:3]| ``randomly'', based on the current time and the processor id.
The array |seed| is suitable for passing to |random_init|.

|call next_seed(n,seed)| advances the seed $n$ step forward.  This
allows independent threads of random numbers to be initialized.

|x = random(rn_args(tag))| which returns the next random number in |x|.
|x| is a |real| random number uniformly distributed in $(0,1)$.

|call random_check(n,rn_args(tag))| which checks that $n$ random numbers
are available for return with |fast_random|. |n| must satisfy |0<= n <=
RAN_K|.

|x = fast_random(rn_args(tag))| which returns the next random number in
|x|.  |fast_random| differs from |random| in that no checking is done
that the random numbers are available.  Thus a call to |random_check|
must precede any calls to |fast_random|.

The random numbers returned by |random| and |fast_random| are of the form
$(i+\frac12)/2^{47}$ for $0\le i < 2^{47}$.

@ Typical calling sequence.  Suppose we are running in a multitasking mode,
then in order that the results be independent of the number of threads
executing the simulation, it is natural to start each flight off with an
independent random number sequence.  The top-level routine would then look
like:

@#if 0
@a
      integer ntasks,nflights,n,num
      rn_seed_decl(seed0)
      rn_seed_decl(seed[0:ntasks-1])
      
      call set_random_seed(seed0)   // Write out the seed!

      num=(nflights+ntasks-1)/ntasks // number of flights/task

      do n=0,nflights-1,num
         rn_seed_copy(seed0,seed0[n/num])
         call next_seed(n,seed[0,n/num])
         call tskstart(xxx,doflights,min(nflights-n,num),seed[0,n/num],xxx)
      end do
@#endif

@ |doflights| would then be defined by

@#if 0
@a
      subroutine doflights(num,seed,xxx)
      rn_decl(x)          // Allocate space for |rn_args|
      integer i,num
      rn_seed_decl(seed)

      do i=0,num-1
         call random_init(seed,rn_args(x)) // Initialize |rn_args|

  /* follow a flight with many calls to |random| or |fast_random| */

        call next_seed(1,seed) // Get next seed
      end do

      return
      end
@#endif

@ The unnamed module.

@a
      @<Functions and Subroutines@>

@ Define constants used in the basic random number generator.
The basic algorithm is
\begin{equation}\label{recursion}X_n=(X_{n-k}-X_{n-j})\bmod 1,\end{equation}
with $k$ and $j$ given by Knuth's Table 3.2.2--1 \cite{knuth2}.  In this
relation, replacing $j$ by $k-j$ is equivalent to running the recursion
backwards.  We make the choice $j\ge k/2$, since this results in longer
vectorizing loops.  We also choose $k=63$, since this is the largest $k$
such that |random_batch| vectorizes with short vector loops.

@m ran_k 63                     // Set $k$.
@m ran_j 31                     // Set $j$

@#if 2*ran_j < ran_k      // and ensure that $j\ge k/2$.
   @m ran_j $EVAL(ran_k-#!ran_j)
@#endif

@m ran_d 25               // $d = \lceil(2-\phi) k\rceil$ ($\phi=\frac12(\sqrt5+1)$) and $\gcd(k,d)=1$

$ASSERT(ran_k == RAN_K)   // Check that the header file set |RAN_K| correctly

@m ran_m0 $EVAL(2^24)                  // The limits on the seeds
@m ran_m1 $EVAL(2^23)

@* Functions to return random numbers.  We calculate the random numbers in
batches, placing the results into the array |ran_array|.  The routine to
return a single random number, |random|, can then merely return the next
array element.  It calls the routine to calculate the next batch if
necessary.  |ran_count| points to the next element of |ran_array| to be
used.  |ran_max| points to the first invalid element of |ran_array|.  Thus
the number of usable random numbers is |ran_max - ran_count|.  |ran_count|,
|ran_max|, and |ran_array| together describe the state of the random number
generator.  The header file packages these together into a single argument
|rn_args(x)|.

@<Functions...@>=
      function random(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      real random               // Function
      rn_decl(x)             // Input/output
      external random_batch     // External

      if (rn_index(x) >= rn_max(x)) then
         call random_batch(rn_args(x))
      end if
      random=rn_array(x)[rn_index(x)]
      rn_index(x)=rn_index(x)+1
      return
      end

@ Check that there are at least |n| random numbers available.  After this
routine the next $n$ random numbers can be retrieved with |fast_random|.
Because of the size of the batches in which the random numbers are
generated, we require $n\le k$.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine random_check(n,rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer n                 // Input
      rn_decl(x)             // Input/output
      external random_batch     // External

      assert(0 <= n && n <= ran_k)
      if (n > rn_max(x)-rn_index(x)) call random_batch(rn_args(x))
      check(n <= rn_max(x)-rn_index(x))
      return
      end

@ If you've called |random_check(n,rn_args(x))|, then you can use this
routine to return the next $n$ random numbers.  Do loops calling
|fast_random| can be vectorized if |fast_random| is expanded inline.

@<Functions...@>=
      function fast_random(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      real fast_random          // Function
      rn_decl(x)             // Input/output

      fast_random=rn_array(x)[rn_index(x)]
      rn_index(x)=rn_index(x)+1
      return
      end

@* Calculate the next batch.  The fills the next $k$ elements of
|rn_array(x)|.  |rn_array(x)| is dimensioned to hold $2k$ elements.  This
simplifies the coding of equation (\ref{recursion}).  It also allows
|random_batch| to be called before the previous batch is exhausted, which
in turn means that we can ensure that at least $n$ elements are available
at any time by calling |random_check(n,rn_args(x))| for $n\le k$.


@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine random_batch(rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      real ran_epsilon
      parameter (ran_epsilon=const(0.5)^48)
      rn_decl(x)             // Input/output
      integer i                 // Local
      real tmp

      check(rn_max(x) == ran_k || rn_max(x) == 2*ran_k) // We assume |rn_max(x)| takes on one of these values

/* First check we didn't take too many random numbers, e.g., by calling
|fast_random| too many times. */
      assert(rn_index(x) >= 0 && rn_index(x) <= rn_max(x))

      if (rn_index(x) < ran_k && rn_max(x) == 2*ran_k) return // Array already full

      if (rn_index(x) >= ran_k && rn_max(x) == 2*ran_k) then // Shift array down
         @<Shift |rn_array(x)| down |ran_k| elements@>
      end if

/* Fill second half.  The compiler codes this as two safe short vectors of
length |ran_j|, in order to avoid the recursion */
      do i=ran_k,2*ran_k-1
         tmp=rn_array(x)[i-ran_k]-rn_array(x)[i-ran_j]
         if (tmp < zero) tmp=one+tmp
         rn_array(x)[i]=tmp+ran_epsilon
      end do

      rn_max(x)=rn_max(x)+ran_k
      rn_count(x)=rn_count(x)+ran_k

      if (rn_index(x) >= ran_k) then // Shift array down
         @<Shift |rn_array(x)| down...@>
      end if

      check(rn_index(x) >= 0 && rn_index(x) < rn_max(x)) // Sanity check on |rn_index(x)|

      return
      end

@ |random_batch| needs to shift the data in the top half of |rn_array(x)| to
the bottom half.

@<Shift |rn_array(x)| down...@>=
         do i=0,ran_k-1         // a short vector move
            rn_array(x)[i]=rn_array(x)[i+ran_k]
         end do
         rn_index(x)=rn_index(x)-ran_k
         rn_max(x)=rn_max(x)-ran_k

@* Initialize random number generator.  This again follows from Knuth
\cite{knuth2}, although the details are taken from the code for Metafont
\cite{knuth86}.  The initialization uses a Fibonacci-like recursion,
\begin{equation} Y_i = Y_{i-2} - Y_{i-1}. \end{equation}
However, this doesn't grow fast enough to fill out all the bits in fraction
of a floating-point number in $k$ iterations since $\phi^k < 2^{47}$
($\phi=\frac12(\sqrt 5 + 1)$).  So we prime it with an extra $k$ iterations.

|seed[i]| (for $i=0,1$) are integer seeds satisfying $0 \le s_0 < 2^{24}$,
$0 \le s_1 < 2^{23}$.  These seeds are used by combining them into a single
47-bit integer $s_0 + s_1 2^{24}$.

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine random_init_old(seed,rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      real ran_delta,ran_epsilon
      parameter (ran_delta=const(0.5)^47,ran_epsilon=const(0.5)^48)
      integer seed[0:1]         // Input
      rn_decl(x)             // Output
      integer i                 // Local
      real k,jj,j
      external random_batch     // External

      assert(0<=seed[0] && seed[0]<ran_m0)
      assert(0<=seed[1] && seed[1]<ran_m1)
      j=(areal(seed[1])*areal(ran_m0)+areal(seed[0]))*ran_delta // starting values
      k=ran_delta
      check(j>=zero && j<one)

      do i=0,ran_k-1            // prime the sequence
         jj=k
         k=j-jj
         if (k < zero) k=k+one
         j=jj
      end do

      do i=0,ran_k-1            // repeat depositing results in |rn_array(x)|
         jj=k
         k=j-jj
         if (k < zero) k=k+one
         j=jj
         rn_array(x)[mod(i*ran_d,ran_k)]=j+ran_epsilon // Shuffle the sequence
      end do

      rn_max(x)=ran_k
      rn_index(x)=ran_k

      do i=1,3                  // Warm up the generator
         call random_batch(rn_args(x))
         rn_index(x)=rn_index(x)+ran_k
      end do

      rn_count(x) = 0           // Initialize count of random numbers produced
      rn_gauss_index(x) = 0     // Initialize the other generators
      rn_iso_index(x) = 0
      rn_cos_index(x) = 0

      end
@#endif

@* Choose random seed depending on current time and processor id.  This
returns the seed as two integers $s_i$ for $i=0,1$.  These seeds are to
be combined into a single 47-bit integer $s = s_0+m s_1$.
We begin by setting the seed to the 14-digit (decimal) number formed by
concatenating the year (mod 100), month, day, hour, minute, second, and
process id (mod 100).  (Note that $2^{47} > 10^{14}$.)

The function |next_seed_old| is then called to randomize the result, so that
successive runs have very different starting seeds.  Within a run,
|next_seed_old| may be called repeatedly to generate a random sequence of
starting seeds.

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine random_seed_old(seed)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer seed[0:1]         // Output
      integer temp[7],i,s[0:1] // Local
      external date_time,get_pid // External
      integer get_pid

      call date_time(temp)
      temp[7]=get_pid()
      goto skip

      entry random_seed_old_test(seed)
      do i=1,7
         temp[i]=99
      end do

skip: continue

      s[0]=0
      s[1]=0

/* These multiplications by $100$ won't give overflow on a 32-bit computer
because $2^{31}/|ran_m0|=128$. */

      do i=1,7
         s[0]=100*s[0]+mod(temp[i],100) // Use last two digits of year and PID
         s[1]=100*s[1]+s[0]/ran_m0
         s[0]=mod(s[0],ran_m0)
         s[1]=mod(s[1],ran_m1)
      end do

      call next_seed_old(1,s,s)

      seed[0]=s[0]
      seed[1]=s[1]

      return
      end
@#endif

@* Generating a sequence of random seeds.  We use the simple linear
congruential formula
\begin{equation}\label{linear}
X_{n+1}=(aX_n + c) \bmod 2^{47},
\end{equation}
See Knuth \cite{knuth2}, Sec.~3.2.1.  We also use the
inverse of equation (\ref{linear})
$$
X_{n-1}=(bX_n + d) \bmod 2^{47},
$$
where $ab = 1 \bmod 2^{47}$ and $d=-bc \bmod 2^{47}$.  We choose $a =
\mathrm{5DEECE66D}_{16} = 273673163155_8 = 25214903917$ and $c = \mathrm
B_{16} = 13_8 = 11$ which are the default choices in the |drand48| library
routine \cite{drand48}.  (|drand48| operates modulo $2^{48}$.  What we are
doing is equivalent to dropping the leading bit.)  We then have $b =
\mathrm{5FE05BCB1365}_{16} = 2776013362611545_8 = 105417217348453$ and $d =
\mathrm{615C0E462AA9}_{16} = 3025601621425251_8 = 107048004364969$.  Since
we want this to work on computers with 32-bit integers, we split up the
47-bit integers into pieces such that $x_0$ holds the low 24 bits and $x_1$
holds to the high 23 bits.

@m ran_a0 0xECE66D
@m ran_a1 0x5DE
@m ran_c0 0xB
@m ran_c1 0x0
@m ran_b0 0xCB1365
@m ran_b1 0x5FE05B
@m ran_d0 0x462AA9
@m ran_d1 0x615C0E

@ |next_seed_old| steps the seed forward $n$ steps.  Thus, if $X_0 = x$, then
we have $X_n = y$.  $n$ is decomposed in binary and $x$ is stepped forward
by powers of 2.  We do this by composing equation (\ref{linear}) with
itself to give, e.g.,
$$
X_{n+2}=(a'X_n + c') \bmod 2^{47}.
$$
where $a'=a^2 \bmod 2^{47}$ and $c'= (a+1)c \bmod 2^{47}$.  The cpu time is
proportional to $\log n$.  Input and output can overlap.

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine next_seed_old(n,x,y)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer n,x[0:1]          // Input
      integer y[0:1]            // Output
      integer a[0:1],c[0:1],t[0:1],m   // Local

      check( 0 <= x[0] && x[0] < ran_m0 )
      check( 0 <= x[1] && x[1] < ran_m1 )

      y[0]=x[0]
      y[1]=x[1]

      if (n == 0) return

      if (n > 0) then
         m = n
         a[0] = ran_a0
         a[1] = ran_a1
         c[0] = ran_c0
         c[1] = ran_c1
      else
         m = -n
         a[0] = ran_b0
         a[1] = ran_b1
         c[0] = ran_d0
         c[1] = ran_d1
      end if

loop: continue
      if (mod(m,2) > 0) then
         call rand_mult(a,y,y)
         call rand_add(y,c,y)
      end if
      m = int(m/2)
      if (m == 0) return
      t[0] = 1                  // Generate coefficients for the square of the mapping.
      t[1] = 0
      call rand_add(a,t,t)
      call rand_mult(t,c,c)     // $c'=(a+1)c$
      call rand_mult(a,a,a)     // $a'=a^2$
      goto loop

      end
@#endif

@ Add two 47-bit numbers, $z=x+y$.  Output can overlap with input.

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine rand_add(x,y,z)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer x[0:1],y[0:1]     // Input
      integer z[0:1]            // Ouput

      z[0]=x[0]+y[0]
      z[1]=mod(x[1]+y[1]+z[0]/ran_m0,ran_m1)
      z[0]=mod(z[0],ran_m0)

      return
      end
@#endif

@ Multiply two 47-bit numbers, $z=xy$.  Output can overlap with input.

@m ran_base 2^12                // The base used in |rand_mult|

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine rand_mult(x,y,z)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer x[0:1],y[0:1]     // Input
      integer z[0:1]            // Ouput
      integer tx[0:3],ty[0:3],tz[0:3],i,j // Local

      do i=0,1
         tx[2*i]=mod(x[i],ran_base)
         ty[2*i]=mod(y[i],ran_base)
         tx[2*i+1]=x[i]/ran_base
         ty[2*i+1]=y[i]/ran_base
      end do

      do i=0,3
         tz[i]=0
         do j=0,i
            tz[i]=tz[i]+tx[j]*ty[i-j]
         end do
      end do

      z[0] = tz[0] + ran_base*mod(tz[1],ran_base)
      z[1] = tz[1]/ran_base + tz[2] + ran_base*mod(tz[3],ran_base)

      z[1] = mod(z[1] + z[0]/ran_m0, ran_m1)
      z[0] = mod(z[0], ran_m0)

      return
      end
@#endif

@* New versions of |random_init|, |set_random_seed|, and |next_seed|.

This initializes the random number array using the high 47 bits of
successive numbers from the |drand48| linear congruential converted to
56 bits.  Define
$$T(x)\equiv (a_1x+c_1) \bmod 2^{56},$$
and
$$T^n(x)\equiv (a_nx+c_n) \bmod 2^{56}.$$
Then we have
$a_1=25214903917$, $c_1=11$;
$a_{63}=53631580600893541$,
$c_{63}=37732446681418217$;
$a_{-63}=39185939196621165$,
$c_{-63}=21393951150075595$.

The 56-bit numbers can be represented as an array of 4 14-bit integers
which allows the multiplications and additions necessary to perform
56-bit multiplies to be carried out on 32-bit machines.  (We don't use
15-bit integers since it is then necessary to do the multiplies more
carefully.)

To generate the seeds for subsequent random number thread we jump the
generator ahead multiples of 63 using $T^{63}$.

@ Here is |random_init|.  This iterates $T$ 63 times.  We make use of
the fact that $a_1$ and $c_1$ are (relatively) small to save on the
number of arithmetic operations here.  Finally the generator is warmed
up by coimputing 10 batches of numbers.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine random_init(seed,rn_dummy(x))
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer base
      real ran_delta,ran_epsilon
      parameter (ran_delta=const(0.5)^14,ran_epsilon=const(0.5)^48,base=2^14)
      rn_seed_decl(seed)        // Input
      rn_decl(x)                // Output
      integer i,j,s[0:3],z[0:3]          // Local
      integer a0,a1,a2,c0,t
      data a0,a1,a2,c0/9837,15283,93,11/ // The base $2^{14}$ representation of $a_1$ and $c_1$

      do i=0,3
         assert(0<=seed[i] && seed[i]<base)
         s[i]=seed[i]
      end do

/* Set |rn_array(x)| from 47 most significant bits of |s|.  We place the
most significant (i.e., the most random) byte of |s| in the least
significant position in |rn_array(x)| where it can do the most good; and
so on with the other bytes; finally we place the five most significant
bits from the least significant byte in the five most significant bit
positions in |rn_array(x)|. */

      rn_array(x)[0]=(((s[3]*ran_delta+s[2])*ran_delta+
     $      s[1])*ran_delta+int(s[0]/512))*const(512.0)*ran_delta+ran_epsilon

      do j=1,ran_k-1
         t=c0+a0*s[0]
         z[0]=mod(t,base)
         t=int(t/base)+a0*s[1]+a1*s[0]
         z[1]=mod(t,base)
         t=int(t/base)+a0*s[2]+a1*s[1]+a2*s[0]
         z[2]=mod(t,base)
         t=int(t/base)+a0*s[3]+a1*s[2]+a2*s[1]
         z[3]=mod(t,base)

         do i=0,3
            s[i]=z[i]
         end do

         rn_array(x)[j]=(((s[3]*ran_delta+s[2])*ran_delta+
     $      s[1])*ran_delta+int(s[0]/512))*const(512.0)*ran_delta+ran_epsilon

      end do

      rn_max(x)=ran_k
      rn_index(x)=ran_k

      do i=1,10                 // Warm up the generator
         call random_batch(rn_args(x))
         rn_index(x)=rn_index(x)+ran_k
      end do

      rn_count(x) = 0           // Initialize count of random numbers produced
      rn_gauss_index(x) = 0     // Initialize the other generators
      rn_iso_index(x) = 0
      rn_cos_index(x) = 0

      end

@* Set the seed from a decimal integer.  |decimal| is a positive decimal
number of arbitrary length (as a character string).  |seed| is set by
taking the low 56 bits of |decimal|.  This is now replaced by the more
general |string_to_seed|.

@<Functions...@>=
@#if 0
      subroutine decimal_to_seed(decimal,seed)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer pow,decbase
      parameter (pow=4,decbase=10^pow)
      character*(*) decimal
      rn_seed_decl(seed)
      integer l,i,a[0:3],c[0:3],o
      data a/decbase,3*0/
      
      l=int((len(decimal)+pow-1)/pow)
      o=1-(pow*l-len(decimal))
      
      do i=0,3
         seed[i]=0
         c[i]=0
      end do
      
      do i=0,l-1
         read(decimal(max(1,o+pow*i):o+pow*(i+1)-1),'(i4)') c[0]
         call rand_axc(a,seed,c)
      end do
         
      return
      end
@#endif

@* Set the seed from an arbitrary string |string| is a string arbitrary
length.  |seed| is initialized to zero. Whitespace is ignored in the
string.  Initial digits 0--9 cause the operation |seed=10*seed+digit|.
Other characters cause the operation |seed=rotr(seed)+digit|, where
|rotr| is a circular right shift by one bit; this is the operation
carried out by the BSD sum utility.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine string_to_seed(string,seed)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer decbase,base
      parameter (decbase=10,base=2^14)
      character*(*) string
      rn_seed_decl(seed)
      logical digits
      integer t,r,i,k,a[0:3],c[0:3],ch
      data a/decbase,3*0/
      
      do i=0,3
         seed[i]=0
         c[i]=0
      end do
      
      digits=.true.
      do i=1,len(string)
         ch=ichar(string(i:i))
         if (digits && ch >= ichar('0') && ch <= ichar('9')) then
            c[0]=ch-ichar('0')
            call rand_axc(a,seed,c)
         else if (!(ch == ichar(' ') || ch == 9)) then // 9 is a tab
            digits=.false.
            seed[0]+=2*ch
            t=mod(seed[0],2)
            r=0
            do k=0,3
               seed[k]=int(seed[k]/2)
               if (k<3) then
                  seed[k]+=mod(seed[k+1],2)*(base/2)+r
               else
                  seed[k]+=t*(base/2)+r
               end if
               r=int(seed[k]/base)
               seed[k]=mod(seed[k],base)
            end do
//       else the character is whitespace and we skip it.
         end if
      end do
         
      return
      end

@* Convert a seed to its decimal equivalent.  |string| should be at
least 17 characters long; otherwise the high digits are lost.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine seed_to_string(seed,string)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer pow,decbase,base
      parameter (pow=4,decbase=10^pow,base=2^14)
      character*(*) string
      rn_seed_decl(seed)
      integer z[0:3],i,t,j,k
      character*20 b
      
      k=-1
      do i=0,3
         z[i]=seed[i]
         assert(0<=z[i] && z[i]<base)
         if (z[i]>0) k=i
      end do
      
      b=' '

 /* $2^{56} < 10^{20}$ */
      
      i=5
loop: continue
      i--
      t=0
      do j=k,0,-1
         z[j]=z[j]+t*base
         t=mod(z[j],decbase)
         z[j]=int(z[j]/decbase)
      end do
      if (z[max(0,k)]==0) k=k-1
      if (k>=0) then
         write(b(pow*i+1:pow*(i+1)),'(i4.4)') t
      else
         write(b(pow*i+1:pow*(i+1)),'(i4)') t
      end if
      if (k>=0) goto loop

      if (len(string)>len(b)) then
         string(:len(string)-len(b)) = ' '
         string(len(string)-len(b)+1:) = b
      else
         string = b(len(b)-len(string)+1:)
      end if
      
      return
      end

@* Choose random seed depending on current time and processor id.  This
initializes the seed with |yyyymmddhhmmsspp| where |yyyy| is the year,
etc. and |pp| is the procoss id (mod 100).

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine set_random_seed(seed)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      rn_seed_decl(seed)        // Output
      integer temp[7]           // Local
      character*17 c
      external date_time,get_pid // External
      integer get_pid

      call date_time(temp)
      temp[7]=mod(get_pid(),1000)
      write(c,'(i4.4,5i2.2,i3.3)') temp
      call string_to_seed(c,seed)

      return
      end

@ |next_seed| steps the seed forward $n$ steps.  Thus, if $X_0 = x$, then
we have $X_n = y$.  $n$ is decomposed in binary and $x$ is stepped forward
by powers of 2.  We do this by composing equation (\ref{linear}) with
itself to give, e.g.,
$$
X_{n+2}=(a'X_n + c') \bmod 2^{56}.
$$
where $a'=a^2 \bmod 2^{56}$ and $c'= (a+1)c \bmod 2^{56}$.  The cpu time is
proportional to $\log n$.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine next_seed(n,y)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer n          // Input
      rn_seed_decl(y)           // Input/Output
      integer af[0:3],cf[0:3],ab[0:3],cb[0:3],a[0:3],c[0:3],z[0:3],t[0:3],m,i
      data af/  101,14806, 6710,12194/ // $a_{63}$
      data cf/ 2537, 9167, 5981, 8579/ // $c_{63}$
      data ab/12653, 2052,13943, 8909/ // $a_{-63}$
      data cb/11979,10897, 6902, 4864/ // $c_{-63}$
      data z/4*0/

      if (n == 0) return

      if (n > 0) then
         m = n
         do i=0,3
            a[i]=af[i]
            c[i]=cf[i]
         end do
      else
         m = -n
         do i=0,3
            a[i]=ab[i]
            c[i]=cb[i]
         end do
      end if

loop: continue
      if (mod(m,2) > 0) then
         call rand_axc(a,y,c)
      end if
      m = int(m/2)
      if (m == 0) return
      rn_seed_copy(c,t)
      call rand_axc(a,c,t) /* $c'=(a+1)c$ */
      rn_seed_copy(a,t)
      call rand_axc(t,a,z) /* $a'=a^2$ */
      goto loop

      end


@ A utility routine.   Implement |x=a*x+c|.

@<Functions...@>=
      subroutine rand_axc(a,x,c)
      implicit_none_f77
      implicit_none_f90
      integer base
      parameter (base=2^14)
      integer a[0:3],c[0:3]     // Input
      integer x[0:3]            // Input/output
      integer z[0:3],i,j,t      // Local
      
      t=0
      do i=0,3
         t=c[i]+t
         do j=0,i
            t+=a[j]*x[i-j]
         end do
         z[i]=mod(t,base)
         t=int(t/base)
      end do
      
@#if SUN
/* The Sun f77 compiler has an optimization bug where it moves the assignment
|x=z| to the previous loop.  This conditional stops this from happening.
Note the |z[0]| should always be non-negative. */
      if (z[0] >= 0) then
@#endif
      do i=0,3
         x[i]=z[i]
      end do
@#if SUN
      end if
@#endif
      
      return
      end

